                "pub_date": 1780979099.973,
                "snippet": "Tier 1 — Highest Priority This is the information the website absolutely needs. Purpose What problem is this trying to solve? Example: Communication barriers Evidence integrity AI diagnostics Governance challenges Mission Why does this project exist? Not what it does. Why it exists. Principles What principles repeatedly appear? Examples: Transparency Honesty Agency Human oversight Auditability Participant-centered design These belong everywhere on the website. Core Concepts Ideas that appear repeatedly. Examples: AI amplifies intent Human validation Continuity Governance Modularity Specialization These become Research or Vision content. Relationship To Ecosystem How does the project connect to everything else? Visitors need to understand: Why isn't this just a random project? Tier 2 — Architecture Information Very important. High-Level Architecture Not code. Not schemas. Not classes. Conceptual architecture. Examples: Human validates output. Evidence remains attached to claims. Specialized nodes perform different roles. Major Components What are the major pieces? For example: TranslAItor Participant Processing Layer Candidate Outputs Human Validation Evidence Analysis Evidence Collection Identity Resolution Quality Assessment Human Review Infrastructure Philosophy This deserves its own category. Look for: Modularity Specialization Resilience Local-first capability Human oversight Tier 3 — Evidence & Credibility Information that demonstrates seriousness. Milestones Examples: First prototype completed. Testing framework built. Infrastructure operational. Current Status What is complete? What is experimental? What is conceptual? Visitors appreciate honesty. Development Progression Important discoveries. Architectural changes. Key decisions. Tier 4 — Research Content These often become website articles. Look for: Recurring Questions Examples: Can continuity fail when alignment succeeds? What is amplified by AI? How should human oversight operate? Frameworks Conceptual models. Not code. Not implementation. The thinking behind the implementation. Cross-Domain Insights Some of your strongest material isn't even AI-specific. Examples: Environmental observations Governance observations System behavior observations These often reveal your broader worldview. Tier 5 — Personal Story Extract sparingly. Only information that explains motivation. Examples: Why transparency matters to you. Why agency matters. Why governance became important. Why you began exploring AI. Avoid collecting: every life event every hardship every anecdote The website is not a memoir. What To Ignore This is equally important. The extraction chat should usually ignore: Detailed Code Unless it explains architecture. File Structures Usually irrelevant. Class Names Usually irrelevant. Implementation Churn \"Version 12 changed field X.\" Not useful. Temporary Decisions Anything later reversed. Debugging Almost always irrelevant. The Final Output Format For every project, I'd want the extraction chat to produce: PROJECT NAME Purpose Mission Problem Addressed Why It Matters Core Principles Architecture Overview Relationship To Ecosystem Current Status Key Milestones Future Direction Website-Worthy Concepts Suggested Public Terminology Information To Exclude That last category is important. The extraction process shouldn't just identify what belongs on the website. It should identify what should remain internal. Because one of the biggest risks with your ecosystem is not lack of content. It's overwhelming visitors with far more detail than they need to understand what you're building.\n\n\nthe next website extraction will be for PEIS\n\n\nPEIS — MULTI-AGENT ENVIRONMENT (FINAL HAND-OFF)\nOBJECTIVE (NON-NEGOTIABLE)\n\nCreate an environment where:\n\nUser + ChatGPT (reasoning) + Local AI (memory/retrieval) interact in a single conversation flow\n\nWith enforced behavior:\n\nLocal AI responds first\nChatGPT refines / corrects\nOutput is structured and usable\nUser remains authority\nCURRENT REAL STATE (VERIFIED)\nInfrastructure\nOpen WebUI is running and accessible\n\nOllama running on XPS at:\n\nhttp://192.168.1.253:11434\n\nLocal model:\n\nqwen2.5:3b\nWhat works\nLocal AI callable via HTTP\nChatGPT callable via API\n\nSequential loop works in terminal:\n\ninput → local → ChatGPT → output\nWhat does NOT work\nInjecting messages into Open WebUI via API\nAttaching to existing chat threads\nUsing /api/chat/completions for UI message control\nUsing /api/chats/{id}/messages (405)\nCRITICAL DISCOVERY (LOCK THIS)\n\nOpen WebUI is NOT designed to accept external message injection into active chats in a stable way.\n\nTherefore:\n\n❌ DO NOT:\n\nAttempt message injection into existing rooms\nAttempt to replay agent messages into UI\nAttempt to control chat state via undocumented endpoints\nCORRECT ARCHITECTURE (MANDATORY)\n\nReplace this:\n\nOpen WebUI → model\n\nWith this:\n\nOpen WebUI → ORCHESTRATOR → (local AI → ChatGPT) → result → Open WebUI\nSYSTEM ROLES\nUser\nAuthority\nCan stop execution at any time\nLocal AI (qwen)\nFirst-pass generator\nProvides draft / raw output\nChatGPT\nEnforces structure\nCorrects / validates output\nFinal authority on format\nOrchestrator (core component)\nControls execution order\nEnforces constraints\nCombines outputs\nReturns final result\nREQUIRED BEHAVIOR\n\nFor input:\n\nrun test suite\n\nSystem must produce:\n\n{\n  \"objective\": \"run test suite\",\n  \"steps\": [\n    { \"action_type\": \"run_pytest\" }\n  ]\n}\n\nNO:\n\nexplanations\nquestions\nconversational fluff\nWHAT MUST BE BUILT (ONLY THIS)\n1. Orchestrator endpoint\n\nA service that:\n\nAccepts:\n\n{ \"message\": \"...\" }\n\nExecutes:\n\nlocal AI → ChatGPT\n\nReturns:\n\nfinal structured output\n2. Open WebUI integration\n\nInstead of calling OpenAI directly:\n\n👉 Open WebUI must call:\n\nhttp://<orchestrator>/v1/chat/completions\n3. Output contract\n\nChatGPT MUST return:\n\nvalid JSON\nno extra text\ndeterministic structure\nHARD RULES (DO NOT VIOLATE)\nDO NOT attempt UI injection again\nDO NOT debug Open WebUI endpoints\nDO NOT create multi-agent loops inside UI\n\nAll coordination happens outside, then returns a single result.\n\nSUCCESS CONDITION\n\nUser types in Open WebUI:\n\nrun test suite\n\nSystem behavior:\n\nRequest goes to orchestrator\nLocal AI runs\nChatGPT refines\nFinal JSON returned\nAppears in UI as normal assistant response\nFAILURE CONDITION\nMultiple messages injected into UI\nAgents appear as separate speakers in UI\nAny reliance on undocumented API routes\nNEXT STEP (FOR NEW CHAT)\n\nRequest:\n\n“Build the orchestrator endpoint and wire Open WebUI to use it instead of OpenAI”\n\nFINAL NOTE\n\nThis is the correct architecture boundary.\n\nPrevious failure was caused by:\n\ntrying to control UI state instead of controlling execution flow\n\n\nI \"REALLY\" dont want to waste more time. no more circles. not more \"what is the strangest way I can make this work\" ideas. if open webui has the ability for my local ai and you to talk together. we need to make it work NOW. if it DOESNT have the capability, then you have hallucinated all of this project and it is worthless.\nThought for 7s\n\nAgreed.\n\nThe hand-off is the boundary now. We do not touch UI injection again. We do not chase Open WebUI internals again. We do not try to make separate speakers appear in the room.\n\nThe only viable next move is the one you locked:\n\nOpen WebUI → orchestrator → local AI → ChatGPT → final result → Open WebUI\n\nThat means the concrete task is:\n\nBuild a small OpenAI-compatible orchestrator endpoint that accepts a normal /v1/chat/completions request, runs:\n\nlocal AI first,\nChatGPT second,\nreturns one final assistant response back to Open WebUI.\n\nThat is also exactly the success condition in your hand-off: user types run test suite, request goes to orchestrator, local AI runs, ChatGPT refines, final JSON appears in Open WebUI as a normal assistant response.\n\nSo the