                "snippet": "This project will become the authoritative **Project Inventory, Services & Product Catalogue** for everything I am currently building, developing, or seriously considering.\n\nThe purpose is to reconstruct and catalogue my complete body of work one project at a time. I will enter each existing ChatGPT Project separately, ask that project to examine its full context, verify my current intent with me, and produce a structured handoff. I will then bring that handoff here so we can build a unified inventory without losing the detailed context or history of the original project.\n\nI also have newer projects and work that currently exist in conversations conducted through a friend’s ChatGPT. I will bring that information here through explanations, and eventually I'll bring documents/hand-offs.\n\nAn important principle governs this catalogue: nearly every project exists because I am building something that I personally need or intend to use. These projects were not invented merely to produce a catalogue of hypothetical products.\n\nThe underlying pattern is:\n\n**A problem I genuinely encounter → a system I need → something I build and use → a reusable foundation → a possible product, service, or commercial opportunity**\n\nCommercial potential is therefore a consequence of the project’s genuine usefulness. It is not the original justification for the project, and it must not replace or distort the project’s real purpose.\n\nFor each project, this catalogue should establish:\n\n* What problem or need originally caused the project to exist\n* What I am actually trying to accomplish\n* Why existing solutions were insufficient for my needs\n* Who the system is initially being built for\n* What has already been designed, implemented, or verified\n* What is currently under development\n* What remains proposed or speculative\n* Which older ideas have been superseded\n* What major components and capabilities the project contains\n* What technical skills and infrastructure it requires\n* How it relates to my other projects\n* How it relates to the larger blAIne ecosystem\n* Which parts may be reusable for other people\n* Who else may experience the same problem\n* What could become a repeatable service\n* What could become a reusable product\n* What could become a custom or client-owned implementation\n* What legitimate revenue models may exist\n* What evidence supports—or does not yet support—the commercial opportunity\n* Where outside expertise could materially strengthen the project\n\nThis catalogue should clearly distinguish among:\n\n* Established purpose\n* Verified implementation\n* Active development\n* Approved future direction\n* Proposed possibilities\n* Experimental or speculative ideas\n* Outdated or superseded concepts\n* Unresolved conflicts requiring my decision\n\nDo not invent commercial readiness, customers, demand, revenue, performance, completion, or capabilities. Do not convert every technical component into a supposed product merely to make the catalogue appear larger. The commercial analysis must remain grounded in what the project genuinely does and what transferable value it may provide.\n\nI am not trying to hide blAIne from anyone. I want the eventual material to explain the big picture: what blAIne is, why these projects connect, how this way of working produces them, and where the larger economic potential may exist.\n\nHowever, explaining blAIne is not the same as inviting people to collaboratively build, govern, or take authority over blAIne. blAIne remains my system and under my direction.\n\nMy DevOps friends are also not the intended customers for DevOps services simply because they work in that field. They already possess formal education and professional expertise. I am not trying to imply that I can improve their own work better than they can.\n\nInstead, I eventually want to show them:\n\n* What I am actually building\n* Why the projects form a coherent body of work\n* How