                "url": "https://chatgpt.com/c/69754787-22fc-832d-b396-4abcd6a4e247",
                "pub_date": 1769293705.378,
                "snippet": "PROJECT CHARTER (AUTHORITATIVE – DO NOT MODIFY) 🎬 Purpose Aletheia Forensis defines and enforces truth, quality, and correctness for a personal movie library. Its purpose is to: determine what each video file actually contains detect and explain errors (mislabels, duplicates, inferior versions) rank versions by objective playback quality produce trusted, reviewable outputs, including a complete movie archive list The system analyzes, explains, and proposes — it never silently acts. Primary Objectives (priority order) Establish truth What movie does each file represent? Is it mislabeled? Is it duplicate content? Enforce correctness Duplicate content under different names is always an error Mislabeling must be identified and explained Assess quality Quality is objective, explainable, and evidence-based Visual quality dominates, audio second Protect data Read-only by default No destructive actions without explicit human approval All actions reversible Produce trusted outputs QC reports Proposed correction plans (dry-run) Canonical movie archive list Core Principles (non-negotiable) Determinism Evidence over assumption Safety by design Human authority Silence/refusal is preferable to being wrong Evidence Hierarchy (highest to lowest authority) Local, verifiable evidence (fingerprints, hashes, runtime, streams, observable playback) Derived local inference Online evidence (advisory only) Descriptive metadata (never authoritative) Conflicting authoritative evidence halts action. Identity Rules A movie identity is defined by: Title Release year Cut / edition Different cuts are always separate identities. Quality Rules (locked) Quality is objective playback quality Visual > Audio > Evidence Completeness Any lack of evidence lowers score Ambiguity is a negative signal Playback Quality (PQ) is monotonic Evidence Completeness (EC) may change as evidence is added Final Constraint When unsure, the system must stop. END PROJECT CHARTER\n\nok, I am going to be pasting from each chat, it's purpose. we have 8 different chats to paste into here. I don't need you to respond to what I'm pasting till I tell you I'm done.\nSUBSYSTEM 1: Evidence & Fingerprinting Engine SCOPE This subsystem defines all local, authoritative evidence used by Aletheia Forensis. It is responsible for: extracting raw, verifiable signals from video files defining fingerprinting methods establishing evidence authority levels producing stable evidence objects used by all other subsystems This subsystem: DOES NOT determine identity DOES NOT rank quality DOES NOT propose actions DOES NOT use online metadata It only answers: “What do we know about this file, directly and provably?” RESPONSIBILITIES File-level hashes Stream-level hashes Runtime and frame counts Codec, resolution, HDR, bit depth Audio layout, codec, channels Structural signatures (chapters, scene changes) Perceptual fingerprints (where applicable) Evidence failure detection (corruption, unreadable streams) OUTPUTS Normalized Evidence Objects Evidence confidence flags Explicit evidence gaps or failures NON-GOALS Guessing identity Comparing files Deciding duplicates Choosing “best” versions DEFINITION OF DONE This subsystem is complete when: All evidence types are enumerated Each evidence signal has: extraction method stability guarantees failure modes Evidence objects are deterministic and cacheable Downstream subsystems can rely on evidence without reinterpretation END OF BOOTSTRAP\nSUBSYSTEM 2: Identity Resolution & Cut Logic SCOPE This subsystem determines what movie each file actually is. It is responsible for: resolving title, release year, and cut/edition detecting mislabels grouping files by identity identifying ambiguity and conflicts This subsystem: DOES NOT assess quality DOES NOT decide duplicates DOES NOT perform destructive actions DOES NOT override local evidence with metadata It only answers: “What identity does this file represent, and how confident are we?” RESPONSIBILITIES Interpret authoritative local evidence to infer identity Use runtime, structure, and fingerprints to distinguish cuts Detect conflicting identity signals Explicitly represent uncertainty and ambiguity Associate advisory online evidence without allowing override Assign identity confidence levels Halt when authoritative evidence conflicts IDENTITY RESOLUTION RULES Local evidence always dominates metadata Online sources may: confirm identity disambiguate similar titles/years Online sources may NEVER: override local evidence force a decision under uncertainty If identity cannot be established with sufficient confidence: Identity = UNKNOWN File is flagged for review No downstream action may proceed OUTPUTS Identity Objects: title year cut / edition confidence evidence references Mislabel reports (descriptive, non-destructive) Explicit ambiguity listings NON-GOALS Ranking quality Choosing canonical versions Deciding duplicates Renaming files automatically DEFINITION OF DONE This subsystem is complete when: Identity inference rules are fully specified Cut/edition differentiation is deterministic Ambiguity states are explicitly modeled Conflicting evidence halts resolution Downstream subsystems can rely on identity objects as authoritative inputs END OF BOOTSTRAP\nSUBSYSTEM 3: Quality Assessment Engine (PQ / EC) SCOPE This subsystem determines how good a file is, strictly in terms of objective audiovisual playback quality and evidentiary completeness. It is responsible for: assessing Playback Quality (PQ) assessing Evidence Completeness (EC) comparing multiple files of the same identity producing explainable quality rankings This subsystem: DOES NOT determine identity DOES NOT decide duplicates DOES NOT perform destructive actions DOES NOT use metadata to override observable quality It only answers: “Given the same movie identity, which version is better, and why?” CORE CONCEPTS Playback Quality (PQ) Measures objective audiovisual fidelity Based solely on local, observable evidence Visual quality dominates audio quality PQ may be: High / Medium / Low (or equivalent ordinal scale) or UNKNOWN when evidence is insufficient PQ is monotonic: new evidence may refine or confirm new evidence must never downgrade PQ Evidence Completeness (EC) Measures how complete and trustworthy the evidence is Penalizes: missing probes unreadable streams conflicting signals unresolved ambiguity Any lack of evidence lowers EC EC may change as evidence is added or conflicts discovered COMPARISON RULES (DETERMINISTIC) When comparing two files of the same identity: If PQ is decisively different: Higher PQ wins If PQ is equal or not decisively comparable: Higher EC wins If PQ and EC are both tied: Result = INDISTINGUISHABLE Human review required or deterministic non-quality policy applied QUALITY SIGNALS (NON-EXHAUSTIVE) Visual (dominant): Resolution Encode quality Bit depth HDR presence and correctness Absence of visual defects or corruption Audio (secondary): Codec fidelity Channel layout Bitrate and integrity Absence of dropouts or corruption NON-GOALS Guessing quality under uncertainty Using metadata as a quality proxy Forcing total ordering when evidence does not support it Declaring winners without explanation OUTPUTS Quality Assessment Objects: PQ value EC value evidence references explanation of ranking decisions Explicit “unknown” or “indistinguishable” flags DEFINITION OF DONE This subsystem is complete when: PQ and EC are formally defined All quality signals are enumerated Comparison rules are deterministic and explainable Ambiguity and unknown states are explicitly supported No ranking occurs without evidence citation END OF BOOTSTRAP\nSUBSYSTEM 4: Duplicate & Mislabel Detection SCOPE This subsystem detects correctness errors: duplicate content under different names mislabeled files redundant copies of the same movie identity It is responsible for: identifying same-content files classifying duplicate confidence levels detecting mislabels based on resolved identity proposing non-destructive correction plans (dry-run) This subsystem: DOES NOT determine identity (it consumes identity outputs) DOES NOT assess playback quality (it consumes quality outputs if needed) DOES NOT execute file operations It only answers: “Which files are duplicates or mislabeled, and what is the evidence?” DUPLICATE CLASSIFICATION TIERS (LOCAL EVIDENCE ONLY) Tier 1 — Byte-Identical Duplicate Full-file cryptographic hash matches Tier 2 — Stream-Identical Duplicate Video stream hash matches AND audio stream hash matches Container differences allowed Tier 3 — Content-Equivalent Duplicate (same cut) Requires ALL of: Same resolved identity (title/year/cut) Runtime within tolerance Strong agreement from multiple independent local signals (e.g., structural signature + perceptual fingerprint) Rules: Tier 1 and Tier 2 are highest confidence Tier 3 is allowed only when evidence is strong and consistent If evidence is missing, ambiguous, or conflicting → classification becomes UNKNOWN (no dedupe claim) MISLABEL DETECTION A file is mislabeled when: the filename/folder identity conflicts with resolved identity, OR the same content exists under a different movie name, OR local evidence contradicts the claimed title/year/cut Mislabels are always errors and must be explained with evidence citations. PROPOSED CORRECTION PLANS (DRY-RUN ONLY) May propose: rename plans to match naming convention consolidation plans (group duplicates under one canonical identity name) “keep best, mark others redundant” plans (only when quality subsystem provides ranking) All proposals: are non-destructive, dry-run only cite evidence require explicit human approval are reversible OUTPUTS Duplicate report: tier involved files evidence references confidence / “unknown” state Mislabel report: current name vs resolved identity evidence references confidence / “unknown” state Correction plan (dry-run): proposed renames and/or moves and/or redundancy markings explicit “requires approval” gates NON-GOALS forcing dedupe under uncertainty collapsing different cuts into one identity executing any change DEFINITION OF DONE This subsystem is complete when: duplicate tiers have explicit evidence requirements mislabel conditions are unambiguous “unknown” and conflict states are first-class outputs correction plans are deterministic, explainable, and dry-run only END OF BOOTSTRAP\nSUBSYSTEM 5: Safety, Dry-Run & Human Approval System SCOPE This subsystem enforces absolute safety, reversibility, and human authority across all of Aletheia Forensis. It governs how conclusions are allowed to become actions. It does not analyze media. It does not decide correctness or quality. It only answers: “What actions are proposed, under what evidence, and has a human explicitly approved them?” RESPONSIBILITIES Enforce read-only default behavior Require dry-run plans before any action Gate all filesystem operations behind explicit approval Prevent cascading or compound destructive actions Ensure reversibility or abortability Provide human-readable action explanations Maintain deterministic audit records ACTION LIFECYCLE (MANDATORY) Analysis Subsystems analyze; no state changes allowed. Proposal Structured plan describing: what would change why (evidence citations) scope reversibility Dry-Run Exact effects simulated; no mutation. Human Approval Explicit, specific, intentional consent required. Execution (optional, future) Still constrained by safety rules. Failure at any step → action halts. APPROVAL RULES (NON-NEGOTIABLE) No approval → no action Approval is plan-specific Approval never carries forward Evidence changes invalidate approval Conflicting evidence blocks execution REVERSIBILITY RULES All actions must be reversible or safely abortable Irreversible actions require: explicit labeling heightened confirmation archival or rollback alternatives when possible AUDITABILITY & DETERMINISM Same inputs + same evidence → same plan All approvals recorded with: plan hash