DESCRIPTORS (NON-AUTHORITATIVE) May be appended only if desired and never used for decisions: resolution HDR flag audio layout source notes Example: Blade Runner (1982) [Final Cut] — 2160p HDR10 Atmos FORBIDDEN NAMING ELEMENTS The following must never be used to imply identity or quality: release group names codec strings bitrate claims subjective labels (“best”, “ultimate”) metadata-only claims AMBIGUOUS / UNRESOLVED ITEMS Files with unresolved identity must not be renamed They remain listed under: UNRESOLVED — Original Filename Ambiguity is preserved, not hidden DETERMINISM RULES Same identity → same canonical name Naming output must be reproducible Naming changes require new dry-run plans and approval OUTPUTS Canonical Movie Archive List Unresolved / Ambiguous Items List Naming Proposals (dry-run only) Evidence references per archive entry NON-GOALS Guessing names Auto-renaming files Using naming as evidence Hiding ambiguity for cleanliness DEFINITION OF DONE This subsystem is complete when: A full archive can be generated from upstream subsystems Naming conventions are deterministic and explicit Unresolved items remain clearly separated No catalog or naming output contradicts evidence Outputs are trusted as final references, not guesses END OF BOOTSTRAP\nSUBSYSTEM 7: Content Filtering & Editing Engine PURPOSE Enable “family-safe” viewing profiles by: producing permanent edited versions (new output files), and/or applying non-destructive playback edits (“edit on the fly”) This subsystem is downstream of truth and quality. It must never alter identity resolution or quality assessment. CORE MODES Mode A — Non-destructive Playback Filtering Skip time ranges Mute time ranges (Optional) Blur/mask time ranges Implemented via a player integration (EDL / scripting / filter pipeline) Mode B — Permanent Edited Outputs Generate a new edited file (never overwrite original) Produce a full dry-run plan first Require explicit human approval Preserve reversibility (original remains intact) EDIT SOURCES (HOW TIMESTAMPS ARE PRODUCED) Manual tagging (highest accuracy) Semi-automatic tagging using subtitles + audio cues Future ML tagging (vision/audio/ASR), always reviewable and evidence-cited SAFETY RULES No edits without a plan and dry-run No destructive overwrite of originals Any uncertain/ambiguous edit recommendation must be flagged for human review The system must be able to explain: what changed, why, and where (timestamps) OUTPUTS Edit Timeline (authoritative for playback): list of events: {start, end, action: skip/mute/blur, reason, confidence} Playback profile presets (e.g., “Mom Mode”, “PG-13-ish”, “No Gore”) Dry-run “edited output plan” (ffmpeg-style cut list, but not executed) Optional “edited file” generation after approval DEFINITION OF DONE This future subsystem is complete when: It can represent edits as deterministic timelines It supports at least skip + mute reliably It can generate a dry-run permanent edit plan It cannot execute anything without explicit approval It integrates cleanly without polluting truth/quality subsystems END OF BOOTSTRAP\nSUBSYSTEM 8: Advisory Intelligence Layer (RAG / LoRA) PURPOSE Add an optional “intelligence layer” that can: retrieve supporting information (RAG) learn patterns for better proposals (LoRA / fine-tuning) This subsystem is STRICTLY advisory. It must never: override local evidence decide identity, duplication, or quality execute actions produce authoritative truth It only produces: candidate hypotheses supporting references draft writeups suggested next evidence to collect AUTHORITY & SAFETY RULES (NON-NEGOTIABLE) Outputs from this subsystem are ALWAYS non-authoritative. Any claim must be labeled as advisory and must cite its sources. If the advisory layer conflicts with local evidence → local evidence wins; advisory is recorded as “conflicting”. No automation chain may depend solely on advisory outputs. Any downstream change still requires dry-run + explicit human approval (Safety subsystem). CORE CAPABILITIES A) RAG: Retrieval-Augmented Generation (Knowledge Assist) RAG may be used for: descriptive catalog enrichment AFTER identity is resolved (cast, crew, synopsis, genres, ratings, external IDs) disambiguation support (advisory only) (similar titles, same-year collisions, known cuts/editions list) “library memory” retrieval from local project artifacts (past QC reports, prior decisions, known tricky titles, your own notes) RAG must not be used for: deciding identity dedupe decisions quality ranking forcing conclusions under uncertainty RAG CACHING & AUDITABILITY All retrieved sources must be cached and recorded. Each retrieved item must include: source identifier retrieval time excerpt hash relevance rationale Outputs must be reproducible from the cached retrieval set. B) LoRA / Fine-Tuning (Optional, Later) LoRA may be considered only to improve proposal quality for: content filtering / family-safe detection suggestions (timestamps) subtitle/OCR/ASR robustness for profanity detection writing consistent structured reports and summaries LoRA must not be used to: decide identity or duplicates rank objective PQ/EC quality “guess” facts without evidence TRAINING DATA POLICY Only train on data you explicitly allow: your own library’s notes / decisions curated labeled clips/timestamps for filtering Training artifacts must be versioned and auditable. Models must be pinned by hash and used deterministically. OUTPUT TYPES Advisory Candidate Identity Matches (non-binding) Advisory Cut/Edition Possibilities (non-binding) Catalog Enrichment Draft Pack (non-authoritative) “Next Evidence to Collect” suggestions Draft human-readable explanations based on authoritative upstream results For filtering: proposed Edit Timeline candidates with confidence labels NON-GOALS autonomous decision making silent actions replacing deterministic evidence extraction replacing objective quality assessment replacing human judgment DEFINITION OF DONE This future subsystem is complete when: RAG retrieval is cacheable, reproducible, and source-cited Advisory outputs cannot override authoritative local evidence LoRA (if used) is versioned, pinned, and strictly proposal-only All outputs integrate without breaking determinism or approval gates END OF BOOTSTRAP\nAdvisory Intelligence Layer (RAG / LoRA) — Project-Level Purpose\n\nAletheia Forensis may optionally include an Advisory Intelligence Layer to assist human understanding, review, and decision-making.\n\nThe purpose of this layer is to:\n\nretrieve and present relevant external or internal knowledge (RAG),\n\nrecall prior project decisions and artifacts,\n\nsuggest hypotheses, explanations, or next evidence to collect,\n\ndraft descriptive or explanatory text based on authoritative upstream results.\n\nThis layer is strictly advisory.\n\nIt must never:\n\nestablish truth,\n\noverride local evidence,\n\ndecide identity, duplication, or quality,\n\nexecute actions,\n\nor act without explicit human approval.\n\nAll outputs from the Advisory Intelligence Layer are non-authoritative proposals that must be:\n\nclearly labeled as advisory,\n\nsource-cited and auditable,\n\nreviewable and rejectable,\n\ninert unless explicitly approved through the Safety & Human Approval system.\n\nThe inclusion of RAG or LoRA does not change the project’s core principles:\n\ndeterminism,\n\nevidence over assumption,\n\nsafety by design,\n\nand absolute human authority.\n\nWhen in conflict with authoritative local evidence, advisory outputs must defer, record the conflict, and stop.\nAt this point you now have all the information regarding this project. I have deleted any previous chats in this project that could have had drift. we now have a complete project, unless of course you now with any new information see any questions or suggestions at a high level to lock down and drill down into?",
